Title: Spawn-based suites sit in check-fast: re-tier the marking drift
Created: 2026-10-02
Author: vibe

--- log ---
2026-10-02T08:42Z vibe created

--- body ---
## Context

`pytest.ini` defines the `integration` marker as "spawns subprocesses or uses
sleep-based timing (excluded from check-fast)". Two suites violate that
definition from inside the fast tier, and they are the fast tier's cost:

- `tests/test_worktree_tools.py` — every test spawns: the `origin` fixture
  runs `git init`/`push`, and each test calls `run()`/`git()` (subprocess).
  26 of its 37 tests carry no `integration` mark (10 later ones do — pure
  marking drift). The unmarked 26 measured **13.55s** — 73% of the entire
  18.4s `make check-fast`.
- `tests/test_adapter_inventory.py` — `collect_nodeids()` runs a full
  `pytest --collect-only` subprocess; its callers are unmarked
  (~1s, second-slowest fast-tier test).

Measured on origin/main 5e600151: `make check-fast` 816 passed in 18.4s;
durations top-12 all `test_gc_*` at ~0.82s. The full `make check` already runs
every test in both files, so re-tiering moves cost out of the edit loop
without growing the pre-PR gate.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Every test in `tests/test_worktree_tools.py` carries the `integration`
      mark (module-level `pytestmark` list alongside the existing skipif),
      with the now-redundant per-test decorators removed
- [ ] The spawn-based tests in `tests/test_adapter_inventory.py` carry the
      `integration` mark
- [ ] `make check-fast` drops to roughly half or less (target: under 10s) and
      still passes
- [ ] Both suites still pass in full `make check`
- [ ] `make check` passes
